---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ProjectMetadata from '../../components/ProjectMetadata.astro';
import ProjectThesis from '../../components/ProjectThesis.astro';
import CaseStudyNav from '../../components/CaseStudyNav.astro';
import DecisionRecord from '../../components/DecisionRecord.astro';
import ArtifactFrame from '../../components/ArtifactFrame.astro';
import Placeholder from '../../components/Placeholder.astro';
import OutcomeSummary from '../../components/OutcomeSummary.astro';
import PrevNext from '../../components/PrevNext.astro';

const sections = [
  { id: 'challenge', label: 'The challenge' },
  { id: 'responsibility', label: 'My responsibility' },
  { id: 'workshops', label: 'Decision workshops' },
  { id: 'decision-system', label: 'Async decision system' },
  { id: 'delivery-reality', label: 'Connecting to delivery' },
  { id: 'produced', label: 'What it produced' },
  { id: 'customer-experience', label: 'Customer experience' },
  { id: 'reflection', label: 'Reflection' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Accessibility Governance and Standards Case Study — Austin Todd"
  description="How Austin Todd helped a cross-functional group turn unresolved accessibility questions into a governance model and published organizational standards."
>
  <article>
    <header class="wrap case-opening">
      <div class="col-reading">
        <p class="case-opening__eyebrow">Case study 01 · Organizational strategy and governance</p>
        <h1 class="case-opening__title">Turning an accessibility commitment into published standards</h1>
        <ProjectThesis>
          I helped a cross-functional group define how accessibility would be governed, tested,
          documented, and escalated across product teams.
        </ProjectThesis>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="wrap">
      <ProjectMetadata
        items={[
          { label: 'Role', value: 'Initiative co-owner, facilitator, and systems designer' },
          { label: 'Collaborators', value: 'Accessibility, UX, development, QA, product, management, and leadership' },
          { label: 'Outcome', value: 'Published organizational accessibility standards and supporting governance structures' },
          { label: 'Scope', value: 'Governance, testing responsibilities, decision-making, measurement, escalation, and exceptions' },
          { label: 'Timeframe', value: '[ADD EXACT TIMEFRAME]' },
        ]}
      />
    </div>

    <div class="wrap case-body">
      <div class="col-reading">
        <CaseStudyNav sections={sections} />
      </div>

      <section id="challenge" class="col-reading case-section" aria-labelledby="challenge-heading">
        <h2 id="challenge-heading" class="case-section__heading">The challenge</h2>
        <p>
          Mouser had committed to improving the accessibility of its web experience, but teams did
          not yet share an operating model for putting that commitment into practice.
        </p>
        <p>Important questions remained unresolved:</p>
        <ul class="case-list">
          <li>Who was responsible for each type of accessibility testing?</li>
          <li>What kinds of product changes triggered manual testing?</li>
          <li>When should testing occur?</li>
          <li>How would teams demonstrate that it had been completed?</li>
          <li>How should incomplete work be escalated?</li>
          <li>
            What happened when accessibility deadlines conflicted with dependencies or business
            priorities?
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          These questions crossed organizational boundaries. UX, development, QA, product,
          accessibility specialists, and leadership each controlled a different part of the
          answer.
        </p>
        <p>
          The organization did not simply need more accessibility documentation. It needed a
          system through which different teams could interpret, apply, and maintain shared
          expectations.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="responsibility" class="col-reading case-section" aria-labelledby="responsibility-heading">
        <h2 id="responsibility-heading" class="case-section__heading">My responsibility</h2>
        <p>
          I worked with our Accessibility Program Manager and my manager, the UX Director, to
          provide the day-to-day structure for the initiative.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Accessibility Program Manager brought deep expertise in accessibility requirements.
          My role was to design the process around that expertise: identify the decisions
          preventing progress, create ways for stakeholders to contribute, facilitate discussion,
          document agreements, and help translate them into governance and standards.
        </p>
        <p>I personally contributed by:</p>
        <ul class="case-list">
          <li>Structuring unresolved questions into decision-focused work</li>
          <li>Designing and facilitating interactive Lucid workshops</li>
          <li>Synthesizing stakeholder input and constraints</li>
          <li>Helping develop governance and responsibility models</li>
          <li>Introducing an asynchronous decision tracker</li>
          <li>Connecting standards discussions to existing delivery workflows</li>
          <li>Supporting the documentation that became the published standards</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The standards were collaboratively developed. I did not act as the sole accessibility
          authority or final decision-maker.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="workshops" class="col-reading case-section" aria-labelledby="workshops-heading">
        <h2 id="workshops-heading" class="case-section__heading">
          I changed the meetings from discussion forums into decision workshops
        </h2>
        <p>
          The initiative involved people with different responsibilities, expertise, and
          incentives. Open discussion could reveal perspectives, but it did not reliably produce
          clear agreements.
        </p>
        <p>
          I designed interactive Lucid activities around specific questions the standards needed
          to answer.
        </p>
        <p>
          One workshop asked stakeholders to assign automated, guided, and advanced manual testing
          to the roles they believed should own each activity. Participants first responded
          independently, making areas of agreement and disagreement visible. I then facilitated
          discussion around the differences.
        </p>
        <p>This surfaced a more complicated reality:</p>
        <ul class="case-list">
          <li>Developers could perform automated checks frequently.</li>
          <li>Front-end developers already performed some manual checks related to their work.</li>
          <li>QA was expected to provide more comprehensive testing.</li>
          <li>Advanced testing depended on knowledge, licenses, and available capacity.</li>
          <li>Not every team had a dedicated front-end specialist.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The operating model needed to reflect actual team conditions—not an idealized
          development process. The workshop did more than collect preferences. It exposed the
          organizational constraints the standards had to accommodate.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="col-wide case-artifact">
        <ArtifactFrame
          status="Placeholder: source artifact needed"
          statusTone="placeholder"
          caption="Sanitized Lucid workshop excerpt: prompt, independent responses, area of disagreement, and resulting direction — with an explanation of what Austin designed versus what the group decided."
        >
          <Placeholder>
            Sanitized Lucid workshop excerpt pending. This will show the testing-ownership prompt,
            independent stakeholder responses, the disagreement it revealed, and the resulting
            direction — not a fabricated reconstruction.
          </Placeholder>
        </ArtifactFrame>
      </div>

      <div class="col-reading case-decision">
        <DecisionRecord
          decision="Assign automated, guided, and advanced manual testing responsibility by activity type and actual team capability, not uniformly across roles."
          evidence="Independent workshop responses exposed disagreement; discussion revealed developers could run automated checks frequently, front-end developers already performed some manual checks, QA was expected to provide more comprehensive testing, and advanced testing depended on knowledge, licenses, and capacity."
          constraint="Not every team had a dedicated front-end specialist, and advanced-testing capacity varied by team."
          direction="Build the operating model around actual team conditions rather than an idealized process."
          status="agreed"
        />
      </div>

      <section id="decision-system" class="col-reading case-section" aria-labelledby="decision-system-heading">
        <h2 id="decision-system-heading" class="case-section__heading">
          I designed a decision system when meetings became the bottleneck
        </h2>
        <p>
          As the work progressed, the group accumulated more unresolved decisions than it could
          address within the remaining meeting time.
        </p>
        <p>
          Adding meetings would have increased the coordination burden without resolving the
          underlying problem. We needed a way to preserve transparency and participation while
          continuing to move.
        </p>
        <p>I helped create an asynchronous decision tracker. Each entry contained:</p>
        <ul class="case-list">
          <li>A focused decision question</li>
          <li>Enough context to respond</li>
          <li>A provisional or default direction</li>
          <li>The stakeholders whose input was especially important</li>
          <li>A response deadline</li>
          <li>A place to provide concerns, reasoning, or an alternative</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          If no supported alternative was submitted by the deadline, the provisional decision
          became the direction the group carried forward.
        </p>
        <p>
          This changed the unit of participation. Instead of reopening an entire topic,
          stakeholders could respond to a specific proposal, explain their concern, and recommend
          an alternative.
        </p>
        <p>
          The tracker did not replace collaborative discussion. It gave the initiative a practical
          way to advance decisions that did not require a full meeting.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="col-wide case-artifact">
        <ArtifactFrame
          status="Placeholder: source artifact needed"
          statusTone="placeholder"
          caption="One authentic decision-tracker entry — question, context, provisional direction, contributors, deadline, and status — sanitized rather than invented."
        >
          <Placeholder>
            Decision tracker excerpt pending. A real entry will be recreated or sanitized here; no
            decision will be invented in its place.
          </Placeholder>
        </ArtifactFrame>
      </div>

      <section id="delivery-reality" class="col-reading case-section" aria-labelledby="delivery-reality-heading">
        <h2 id="delivery-reality-heading" class="case-section__heading">
          I helped connect the standards to delivery reality
        </h2>
        <p>
          The exception process forced the group to reconcile organizational expectations with
          product-delivery constraints.
        </p>
        <p>
          A team might understand the applicable standard and still be unable to meet its deadline
          because of a technical dependency, competing priority, third-party system, or roadmap
          decision.
        </p>
        <p>
          The exception could not mean that accessibility was no longer required. It needed to
          document why the timeline had changed, preserve visibility, and route priority conflicts
          to the people authorized to make them.
        </p>
        <p>The group explored:</p>
        <ul class="case-list">
          <li>When teams should raise an exception</li>
          <li>What information needed to be recorded</li>
          <li>Who should review it</li>
          <li>How leadership should gain visibility</li>
          <li>How follow-up should occur</li>
          <li>Whether exceptions should live in a separate reporting system or existing delivery tooling</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          A separate tool could simplify centralized reporting but create another disconnected
          workflow. Capturing the information near the work could reduce duplication but required
          a reliable escalation path.
        </p>
        <p>
          We moved toward a process that kept the underlying accessibility work open, documented
          the reason for the timeline conflict, and made the exception visible for product and
          leadership discussion.
        </p>
        <p>
          This distinguished an exception to the delivery timeline from an exception to the
          accessibility expectation itself. The first could be managed transparently. The second
          was not the purpose of the process.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="col-wide case-artifact">
        <ArtifactFrame
          status="Anonymized reconstruction"
          statusTone="placeholder"
          caption="Anonymized reconstruction of the proposed exception flow: team, product owner or manager, leadership decision point, documentation, and follow-up loop. Directional only — confirm the final flow before treating any state as implemented."
        >
          <div class="flow">
            <ol class="flow__steps">
              <li class="flow__step">Team raises exception</li>
              <li class="flow__step">Product owner / manager reviews</li>
              <li class="flow__step">Leadership decision point</li>
              <li class="flow__step">Documentation stays open</li>
              <li class="flow__step">Follow-up loop</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </ArtifactFrame>
      </div>

      <div class="col-reading case-decision">
        <DecisionRecord
          decision="An exception applies to the delivery timeline, never to the accessibility expectation itself."
          evidence="Teams could understand a standard and still miss its deadline because of a technical dependency, competing priority, third-party system, or roadmap decision."
          constraint="A separate tool could simplify centralized reporting but create a disconnected workflow; capturing information near the work required a reliable escalation path."
          direction="Keep the underlying accessibility work open, document the reason for the timeline conflict, and make the exception visible to product and leadership."
          status="proposed"
        />
      </div>

      <section id="produced" class="col-reading case-section" aria-labelledby="produced-heading">
        <h2 id="produced-heading" class="case-section__heading">What the work produced</h2>
        <p>The initiative resulted in published accessibility standards supported by:</p>
        <ul class="case-list">
          <li>Defined testing expectations</li>
          <li>More explicit role responsibilities</li>
          <li>A governance structure</li>
          <li>A mechanism for advancing unresolved decisions</li>
          <li>A developing escalation and exception process</li>
          <li>Documentation intended to connect standards to product delivery</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Publication changed the state of the work from a collection of unresolved discussions to
          a documented organizational direction.
        </p>
        <p>
          It did not, by itself, prove complete adoption. The next measure of success is whether
          teams can find, understand, apply, and maintain the standards—and whether the
          organization can see where the operating model is working or breaking down.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="col-reading case-outcome">
        <OutcomeSummary
          verified="Standards were published and supported by governance, responsibility, decision, and exception structures."
          unverified="Universal adoption, workflow compliance, defect reduction, or customer impact."
        />
      </div>

      <section id="customer-experience" class="col-reading case-section" aria-labelledby="customer-experience-heading">
        <h2 id="customer-experience-heading" class="case-section__heading">
          Why it mattered to the customer experience
        </h2>
        <p>
          Customers do not experience an accessibility policy. They experience individual pages,
          components, forms, and interactions produced by different teams over time.
        </p>
        <p>
          If those teams use inconsistent testing practices or unclear ownership, experience
          quality becomes dependent on who happened to work on a particular feature.
        </p>
        <p>
          The governance model was intended to reduce that inconsistency by making accessibility
          part of a shared delivery practice rather than relying exclusively on specialist
          intervention.
        </p>
        <p>
          We have not yet established evidence that the published standards improved customer
          outcomes. That is an important distinction between the intended effect of the system and
          its currently verified outcome.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="reflection" class="col-reading case-section case-section--reflection" aria-labelledby="reflection-heading">
        <h2 id="reflection-heading" class="case-section__heading">Reflection</h2>
        <ProjectThesis>
          This project changed how I think about standards and governance.
        </ProjectThesis>
        <p>
          A standard is itself an experience. Its users need to understand what applies to them,
          what they are responsible for, how to demonstrate completion, and what to do when
          reality does not match the intended process.
        </p>
        <p>
          Correct content is necessary, but it is not sufficient. The system surrounding the
          standard determines whether an organizational commitment can become repeatable action.
        </p>
        <p>
          My strongest contribution was designing that surrounding system: framing the necessary
          decisions, creating structures for participation, and helping move the organization from
          broad intention to published direction.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <div class="wrap">
      <PrevNext
        next={{
          label: 'Next case study',
          title: 'Making UX impact measurable',
          href: '/work/ux-measurement-framework',
        }}
      />
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .case-opening {
    padding-block: var(--space-6) var(--space-4);
  }

  .case-opening__eyebrow {
    font-size: var(--step-meta);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--color-blue);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  .case-opening__title {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: var(--step-case-title);
    line-height: 1.1;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .case-body {
    padding-top: var(--space-5);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }

  .case-section {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .case-section > p,
  .case-section > ul {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  .case-section__heading {
    font-size: var(--step-section);
    color: var(--color-text);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-1);
  }

  .case-section--reflection .case-section__heading {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
  }

  .case-list {
    list-style: disc;
    padding-left: 1.4em;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5em;
  }

  .case-artifact,
  .case-decision,
  .case-outcome {
    margin-inline: auto;
    width: 100%;
  }

  .flow {
    width: 100%;
  }

  .flow__steps {
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    counter-reset: step;
  }

  .flow__step {
    border: var(--border-thin);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    padding: 0.6em 0.9em;
    font-size: var(--step-meta);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-align: center;
    position: relative;
  }

  .flow__step:not(:last-child)::after {
    content: '';
    display: inline-block;
    width: var(--space-3);
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--color-border);
    margin-left: var(--space-2);
    vertical-align: middle;
  }

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .flow__steps {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: stretch;
    }

    .flow__step:not(:last-child)::after {
      display: none;
    }

    .flow__step:not(:last-child) {
      margin-bottom: var(--space-1);
    }
  }
</style>
